title: Backups and restore
tags: Storage
kind: guide
summary: A verified copy of a repository's store, taken while it stays loaded — daily and on demand — and how to put one back.

!! Taking a backup

```
mf -n music repo backup                  # under .metafolder/internal/backups/manual-<time>/
mf -n music repo backup --to /mnt/usb/music-backup
```

A backup is a consistent copy of the store, taken while the daemon runs (LMDB's
compacting hot copy), with the repository's `config.json`, its configuration's
`config/schema.json` and `config/metadata-map.toml`, and a
`backup.json` (when, which store, how many metarecords). It is written under a
temporary name, then opened and checked before it takes its place: a store damaged
since the last backup fails its new one, which then never replaces a good one.
`--to` must name a new directory; by default the directory is always a new one too (a
second backup within the same second is `manual-<time>-2`).

The daemon also backs up each loaded repository ''automatically'', every
`auto-backup-days` days (default 7; `0` turns it off — [[Daemon configuration]]),
into one slot, `internal/backups/auto/`, looking at startup and then every hour. A
failure is a warning in the repository's diagnostics. A backup holds the repository
only while it copies the store — every other request waits meanwhile — and checks the
copy after letting go of it. Either is a `backup` task while it runs, so the GUI's
task bar shows it ([[Task kinds]]); the automatic one is logged as `backup.auto` when slow
([[What the slow log records]]).

!! Restoring

```
mf -n music repo restore                      # the most recent backup
mf -n music repo restore --from <dir>
mf repo restore --path /data/music            # a repository that no longer loads
mf repo restore --metafolder <dir>            # … with an external .metafolder
```

The usual reason to restore is a store that no longer loads, hence the forms naming
a repository by its path. A loaded repository is unloaded, restored and loaded back;
an unloaded one is loaded once restored.

The backup is `--from`, or the most recent under `internal/backups/` (by the time in
its `backup.json`; a directory without one is not a backup). It must be the ''same''
repository — its `config.json` names the same `repo_uuid` — or it is refused. It is
copied beside the current store, opened and checked (a backup that no longer checks
clean changes nothing); then the current store, if any, is set aside as
`internal/pre-restore-<time>-<name>` — the daemon never deletes it — and the copy
takes its place. An interruption leaves the old store set aside, and restoring again
completes it. A lost `config.json` is replaced by the backup's; `config/` is not
touched (it is ordinary content — the backup's copies are there to take by hand).

''Everything written since the backup is lost'': your edits, and the file changes the
watcher recorded. The command says when the backup was taken and advises
`mf reconcile` to catch up with the files ([[Running a reconcile]]); it does not run it.

A repository whose store is missing does not load at all — once, a load created an
empty store in its place, a reconcile filled it, and the automatic backup then
replaced the last good one. Restoring by path is how it comes back.
